{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 17, :comments ["<b>You may not.</b> Rashi explains: Rabbi Yehoshua ben Korchah says — you are able, but you are not permitted. Similarly: 'et the Jebusite,' etc. — because Abraham made a covenant with them when he bought from them the Cave of Machpelah. They were not Jebusites but Hittites; rather, they were called so after the name of the city, whose name was Jebus; so it is explained in Pirkei deRabbi Eliezer. And what Rashi explained in the book of Samuel, that 'et the Jebusite' were of the seed of Avimelech — that is not according to Rabbi Yehoshua, even though it is written: 'if you deal falsely with me and my descendants and my posterity,' and those generations had already passed in the days of David — nevertheless, they would see the oath written on the Paschal lambs and assumed that the oath was still in force." "<b>To eat within your gates, the tithe of your grain.</b> With the Levites it speaks of the first tithe, and with Israelites of the second tithe." "<b>And the firstborn of your herd and your flock.</b> But is the firstborn eaten only by the priests? And this verse is written concerning Israelites! Rather, they would set aside from it two offspring: the firstborn of the animal to the priest, and the second to themselves, and the practice of Israelites was to eat their own portion in Jerusalem, like the second tithe. Another interpretation: the verse speaks of firstborn females, and the practice of Israelites was to eat them in Jerusalem. And in the Yerushalmi there is a dispute between these two interpretations. Another interpretation: the verse speaks of the firstborn itself, and it speaks concerning the priests, for the priests and also the Levites are included in Israel — as in 'before YHWH your Elohim you shall eat it,' which means: whoever is obligated to eat it."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :chizkuni, :lang :en, :chapter 12, :notes {0 "פרש״‎י refers to Rashi's commentary. The reference to the oath written on Pesach lambs alludes to Genesis 21's covenant with Avimelech; the mechanical resolution of מ״‎מ as 'nevertheless' is standard."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:52:39.559998669Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}